---
mm: 0.1
kind: doc
name: modules.cpp abstract data model
---

# Purpose
models/ is an abstract C++20 module hierarchy that models this project's own
manifest-driven structure, tools, workflow, and generated output as data. It
is a second, independent description of the same facts docs/modules.mdy
gives in prose: an abstract interface a program can query, rather than a
document a person reads. mm.model (modules/mm/model) adapts the real project
onto that interface, and tools/model uses it to check the project against
itself.

Like everything else in the project, models/ follows docs/modules-c++20.mdy:
modules instead of headers, no exceptions, no new templates, enum class,
[[nodiscard]] on accessors, and explicit virtual destructors on every base.

# The eight modules
Eight modules, each under models/<name>/<name>.cppm with its own mm.mdy, in
dependency order:

- models.document — front matter and body blocks for one MDY file
  (models::Document, models::Block). Foundational: every MDY file is a
  document, including a manifest, so everything else sits above this.
- models.configuration — a compiler configuration (models::Configuration),
  mirroring mm::build::Toolchain. Foundational and independent: a
  Configuration is an input to running a tool, not a structural fact about
  the repository.
- models.manifest — the manifest kind taxonomy docs/modules.mdy defines:
  project, dir, module, app, test, doc (models::Kind, models::ManifestNode,
  models::BuildableNode, and one class per kind). Imports models.document,
  because every manifest is one.
- models.repository — the aggregate view over a whole manifest tree
  (models::Repository): a project's root plus every module, app, test, and
  doc reachable from it. Imports models.manifest.
- models.tool — an invocable tool (models::Tool), distinct from
  models::ModuleNode's declaration: a Tool may have no declaring manifest at
  all, such as a third party tool or the pre-manifest bootstrap artifacts
  build0/build1. Imports models.manifest.
- models.module — a resolved C++20 module (models::Module), distinct from
  models::ModuleNode the same way: ModuleNode's uses() is dependency names as
  text, Module's imports() is those names resolved to other Module objects.
  Imports models.manifest.
- models.workflow — a workflow step (models::Operation): one of the top
  level *.sh scripts, its prerequisites, whether it is optional, and the
  Tools it drives, in order. Most operations drive exactly one; test.sh
  drives several. Imports models.tool.
- models.artifacts — a generated artifact (models::GeneratedArtifact,
  models::ArtifactKind), starting from docs/modules.mdy's "Generated
  artifacts" section but splitting AppObject and ToolObject each into an
  object-file and an executable variant, plus Staged for out/build0 and
  out/build1, and the Operation whose script produces it. Imports
  models.workflow.

# Design choices worth knowing
- Every module is a single export module, not one class per module,
  following mm.build's own precedent of bundling a tightly coupled family of
  types together rather than splitting each class out.
- A declaration is not the thing it declares. models::AppNode and
  models::Tool both exist because a manifest saying an app should exist is
  not the same fact as a binary existing to run; models::ModuleNode and
  models::Module split the same way, for uses: as text versus imports() as a
  resolved graph. This is the recurring shape across models.tool and
  models.module.
- Nothing here executes anything. There is deliberately no run() on Tool or
  Operation: invoking things is mm.build's and mm.test's job. models/
  describes what exists and how it relates, not how to act on it.

# mm.model: the adapter
modules/mm/model adapts mm::build's and mm::mdy's real data onto
models::Repository and models::Tool, rather than duplicating either. Its one
exported type, mm::model::Loaded, owns every concrete node, document, and
tool it builds, so pointers returned through the models:: interfaces stay
valid for Loaded's lifetime.

Loaded::tools() gives one models::Tool per kind:app manifest, each
Provenance::BuiltIn with declared_by() pointing at that app's AppNode and
invocation() set to out/bin/<name>, matching what build.sh actually
installs. build0, build1, and third party tools (cppcheck, semgrep) are not
represented: none has a declaring manifest this adapter can walk to, so
populating them would mean inventing data rather than adapting it.

`Loaded::load(root, ok)` walks the project the same way tools/check does:
the manifest tree from root, plus the tests/ subtree loaded separately,
since the root manifest has no folder: tests entry. Each node is given a
real document() by re-parsing its own mm.mdy through mm::mdy::Parser, since
mm::build::Target does not keep the MDYDocument it read.

This adapter does not yet resolve ManifestNode::parent() or children():
every node it builds returns nullptr or empty for those, because
mm::build::load_tree's Target does not carry the structural nesting
mm::build::Node does. Filling them in would mean also walking load_nodes and
cross-referencing the two by directory; nothing that currently imports
mm.model needs it.

# tools/model: the consistency tool
tools/model is a kind:app target, use: mm.model, installed as out/bin/model.
It checks the real project against itself through models::Repository and
models::Tool alone, never touching mm::build's structures directly, so what
it reports is whatever the abstract model itself would tell a reader:

- dependency resolution — every use: entry on a module, app, or test names a
  module: some kind:module manifest actually exports. mm::build::order
  already rejects an unknown module at build time; this re-derives the same
  fact independently, through the model, as a check that does not require a
  full build to run.
- build completeness — every models::Tool's invocation() exists under
  out/bin: what is declared matches what has actually been built. Because
  this is expressed over Tool rather than AppNode directly, the same check
  would cover build0, build1, or a third party tool too, the day mm.model
  represents them.

Common commands are:

- ./out/bin/model
- ./out/bin/model -v
- ./out/bin/model modules/mm.mdy

Both checks are structural, derived from models::Repository, rather than
textual parsing of docs/modules.mdy's prose. A textual check against that
prose (for example, "is every *.sh script mentioned in the Repository layout
tree") is future work, not something tools/model does today.

# Boundaries
- models/ has no children()/parent() resolution yet, as above; nothing here
  currently needs a full structural walk, only the flat sets
  Repository::modules()/apps()/tests()/docs() already provide.
- models.workflow and models.artifacts have no adapter yet: mm.model builds
  models.document, models.manifest, models.repository, and, for kind:app
  manifests only, models.tool. A tool that needs Operation or
  GeneratedArtifact data must extend mm.model first.
- mm.model's models.tool coverage is manifest-only: build0, build1, and
  third party tools such as cppcheck and semgrep are real Tools this project
  uses but have no kind:app manifest, so they are absent from
  Loaded::tools() rather than represented with a null declared_by().
- tools/model does not check docs/modules.mdy's prose against reality (for
  example, its Repository layout tree, or the Build and development
  workflow script list); it checks only what models::Repository can already
  answer.
